{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgP3hYniKHhdxGvt31o1v","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I ruled RB1-F1 SUCCESSOR. The verdict is at `docs/reviews/wi-976/004-ADJUDICATE-81e623b.md`, committed once as 4020f97a5 with `WI: WI-976` and `Loop-Session: 20261025T055732Z-64b2b6` in one trailer block.\n\n**`RULING: RB1-F1 SUCCESSOR`**\n\n**The finding is real.** I reproduced it with the lane's own `sitting_hat_block`. The in-lane route passes the authoring work item's row, so its tags shape the hat set. The merge-mint route passes the minted adjudication row, which is hard-coded to `process` and doesn't point back to the authoring work item. So the same SR gets different hats:\n\n| SR | Lane tag | In-lane route | Merge-mint route |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| SR-157 | `scripts` | asks CROSS-PLATFORM | doesn't |\n| SR-161 | `unattended` | asks UNATTENDED-OPS | swaps it for FIRST-RUN-ADOPTER |\n\nThe merge-mint route is the kit's shipped loop path, so PROCESS.md §6's bound doesn't exclude it.\n\n**It isn't convergence-class, so SUCCESSOR is allowed:**\n- No code gates on the hat block; by the ruling, a missing outcome is a review finding, not a failed check.\n- Nothing is lost.\n- The Done-when is met: each route widens by the row it's handed, every tested state holds, and this lane's own sitting composes in-lane.\n\n**Why not fix it here:**\n- The cheap remedy, making the in-lane route use the minted row shape, settles on the less informed hat set.\n- The proper remedy changes the merge mint in `intake.py`, which is outside this lane's declared surface. It needs a new cell for the lane's tags, since `SafetyClass` already routes minted rows, and an answer for the branchless sweep.\n\n**What the successor row must do:**\n- carry the authoring lane's tags onto minted first-approval and amendment rows;\n- resolve the branchless sweep;\n- state in LLR-360 that the authoring work item's tags are the ones that widen